## Slide 1

Payload Definitions

## Slide 2

Overview

Payload Definitions

The Payload Definition Hub can be accessed from the Developer page, under the Connect Hub. 
Payload Definitions can be nested in a Group folder for organization and maintainability.
Payload Definitions consist of header information as well as Field Definitions.
Payload Definitions and their Field Definitions can be manually created or imported if the user has an example message file (CSV, JSON, etc..).

## Slide 3

Common Configuration

Payload Definitions

Name
Name of Payload Definition (non-programmatic)

Key
Unique identifier of the Payload Definition. This is important as it will be used to reference the Payload Definition during data processing.

Enabled
Whether or not the Payload Definition is enabled and accessible

## Slide 4

File Type Dependent Configuration

Payload Definitions

Line Separator (CSV)
Option for new line in the CSV file. \r\n and \n supported
Column Separator (CSV)
Text field for the column delineator in the CSV file
Header Line (CSV)
Definition of the column numbers in the file. Example would be 0,2,4,5
First Row Contains Header (CSV)
Whether or not the first row of the CSV file contains column headers
Root List (JSON)
When true, this JSON payload is expected to contain a list of objects at the root level.
Root Content (XML)
Defines the root node of the XML data. This becomes more important if TrakSYS is sending the Payload, as this content gets added to the file when it’s built.

## Slide 5

Field Definitions

Payload Definitions

Generate Automatically via Import
Payload Definitions and their Fields can be generated automatically via the Import option. Fields will be automatically assigned a data type which can later be changed through configuration.
Create Manually
Instead of importing a sample payload, a Payload Definition can be manually created, and Field Definitions can be manually added to it. 
Nesting
Payload definitions support field nesting

## Slide 6

Field Definition Configuration

Payload Definitions

Name
Name of the field (non-programmatic)
Key
Unique identifier of the field. This is important as it will be used to reference the field in script.
List
Whether of not the field is a list node
Source Path
Specifies the actual field name in the payload
Required Mode
Whether or not the field is required to be included in the payload
Data Type
Type of data: Object, String, Integer, Double, Boolean, Date Time, Date Time Offset, Enum, Long
Format String
Applies specific string formatting to the data. Commonly use with dates